  ($q$alg-540$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear. If f(3) = −4 and f(6) = −7, what is the value of f(1)?$q$, $q$["−2","−3","−1","0"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The slope is (−7 − (−4))/(6 − 3) = −1, and f(0) = −1. So f(1) = −1(1) − 1 = −2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-541$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A taxi company charges $6.00 for the first mile and $1.00 for each additional mile. A passenger paid $19.00 for a ride. How many miles was the ride? (Assume the ride covered a whole number of miles.)$q$, $q$["19","12","15","14"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first mile costs $6.00, leaving $19.00 − $6.00 = $13.00 for additional miles: 13 additional miles at $1.00. Total distance = 1 + 13 = 14 miles.$q$),
  ($q$alg-542$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$2x + 6y = 13 kx + 12y = 41 In the system above, k is a constant. For what value of k does the system have no solution?$q$, $q$["8","2","12","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$No solution requires parallel lines: the ratios of x- and y-coefficients match but the constants do not. k/2 = 12/6, so k = 4. (The constants 13 and 41 are not in that same ratio, so the lines are distinct.)$q$),
  ($q$alg-543$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A ticket office sold 84 adult and student tickets for a school play in one day. Adult tickets cost $10 each, and student tickets cost $5 each. If the total revenue was $585, how many adult tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["28","33","51","74"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let a be the number of adult tickets. Then a + s = 84 and 10a + 5s = 585. Substituting s = 84 − a gives 5a = 165, so a = 33.$q$),
  ($q$alg-544$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A tank contains 515 gallons of water and drains at a constant rate of 10 gallons per minute. After how many minutes will the tank contain 265 gallons?$q$, $q$["26","25","30","250"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$515 − 10t = 265 → 10t = 250 → t = 25.$q$),
  ($q$alg-545$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$How many integers x satisfy −8 < x + 2 ≤ 5?$q$, $q$["12","15","13","14"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Subtract 2: −10 < x ≤ 3. The integers are −9 through 3, which is 13 integers.$q$),
  ($q$alg-546$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A ticket office sold 81 adult and student tickets for a school play in one day. Adult tickets cost $12 each, and student tickets cost $7 each. If the total revenue was $847, how many adult tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["51","61","56","25"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let a be the number of adult tickets. Then a + s = 81 and 12a + 7s = 847. Substituting s = 81 − a gives 5a = 280, so a = 56.$q$),
  ($q$alg-547$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Line ℓ is perpendicular to the line y = 5x + 1 and passes through the point (2, 5). What is the slope of line ℓ?$q$, $q$["5","1/5","−1/5","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals. The given slope is 5, so ℓ has slope −1/5. The point does not change the slope.$q$),
  ($q$alg-548$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations below has solution (x, y). 2x − y = −9 3x + 2y = 4 What is the value of x + y?$q$, $q$["−2","4","5","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Solving the system gives x = −2 and y = 5, so x + y = 3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-549$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 4x − 11 = −3, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["−3","8","−2","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$4x = −3 + 11 = 8, so x = 2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-550$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear. If f(0) = 4 and f(2) = 8, what is the value of f(5)?$q$, $q$["10","6","14","−14"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The slope is (8 − 4)/(2 − 0) = 2, and f(0) = 4. So f(5) = 2(5) + 4 = 14.$q$),
  ($q$alg-551$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations below has solution (x, y). 4x − 5y = −51 3x − 3y = −33 What is the value of y?$q$, $q$["−7","8","−28","7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Solving the system gives x = −4 and y = 7, so y = 7.$q$),
  ($q$alg-552$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 6 and x − y = −2, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["4","−2","6","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Subtract the equations: 2y = 8, so y = 4.$q$),
  ($q$alg-553$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The line with equation −3x + 3y = −9 crosses the x-axis at (p, 0) and the y-axis at (0, q). What is the value of p + q?$q$, $q$["6","0","1","−9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Setting y = 0 gives p = 3. Setting x = 0 gives q = −3. So p + q = 0.$q$),
  ($q$alg-554$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A ticket office sold 47 adult and student tickets for a school play in one day. Adult tickets cost $10 each, and student tickets cost $7 each. If the total revenue was $428, how many adult tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["28","33","14","37"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let a be the number of adult tickets. Then a + s = 47 and 10a + 7s = 428. Substituting s = 47 − a gives 3a = 99, so a = 33.$q$),
  ($q$alg-555$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The line with equation −12x + 14y = −84 crosses the x-axis at (p, 0) and the y-axis at (0, q). What is the value of p + q?$q$, $q$["−1","−84","−42","1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting y = 0 gives p = 7. Setting x = 0 gives q = −6. So p + q = 1.$q$),
  ($q$alg-556$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 4x − 2 > 29?$q$, $q$["7","8","10","9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$4x > 31, so x > 7.75. The smallest integer greater than 7.75 is 8.$q$),
  ($q$alg-557$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which ordered pair (x, y) is a solution to the inequality 5x + y < 9?$q$, $q$["(8, 3)","(2, 1)","(3, 2)","(−2, −2)"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only (−2, −2) works: 5(−2) + 1(−2) = −12, which is less than 9. The other pairs give values of 9 or more.$q$),
  ($q$alg-558$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line that passes through the points (−1, −2) and (1, 10)?$q$, $q$["6","5","7","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (10 − (−2)) / (1 − (−1)) = 12/2 = 6.$q$),
  ($q$alg-559$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line with equation 3x + 3y = 50?$q$, $q$["−1","3","1","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solve for y: y = −3/3 x + constant, so the slope is −1.$q$),
  ($q$alg-560$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Line ℓ is perpendicular to the line y = 2x + 1 and passes through the point (−4, 3). What is the slope of line ℓ?$q$, $q$["1/2","2","−1/2","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals. The given slope is 2, so ℓ has slope −1/2. The point does not change the slope.$q$),
  ($q$alg-561$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line that passes through the points (3, −3) and (5, −1)?$q$, $q$["−1","1","2","0"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slope = (−1 − (−3)) / (5 − 3) = 2/2 = 1.$q$),
  ($q$alg-562$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations below has solution (x, y). 2x + 3y = 0 x + 5y = −14 What is the value of x?$q$, $q$["7","−4","6","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Solving the system gives x = 6 and y = −4, so x = 6.$q$),
  ($q$alg-563$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A gym charges a one-time sign-up fee of $58 plus $19 per month. If a member has paid a total of $381 so far, for how many months has the member been enrolled?$q$, $q$["323","17","18","20"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$58 + 19m = 381 → 19m = 323 → m = 17.$q$),
  ($q$alg-564$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Line ℓ is perpendicular to the line y = 2x − 4 and passes through the point (5, 1). What is the slope of line ℓ?$q$, $q$["−1/2","2","1/2","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals. The given slope is 2, so ℓ has slope −1/2. The point does not change the slope.$q$),
  ($q$alg-565$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 13 and x − y = 1, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["13","7","6","1"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Add the equations: 2x = 14, so x = 7.$q$),
  ($q$alg-566$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The line with equation −5x + 2y = −10 crosses the x-axis at (p, 0) and the y-axis at (0, q). What is the value of p + q?$q$, $q$["7","−3","−10","3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Setting y = 0 gives p = 2. Setting x = 0 gives q = −5. So p + q = −3.$q$),
  ($q$alg-567$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A taxi company charges $3.00 for the first mile and $2.00 for each additional mile. A passenger paid $23.00 for a ride. How many miles was the ride? (Assume the ride covered a whole number of miles.)$q$, $q$["9","10","11","12"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first mile costs $3.00, leaving $23.00 − $3.00 = $20.00 for additional miles: 10 additional miles at $2.00. Total distance = 1 + 10 = 11 miles.$q$),
  ($q$alg-568$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$In the equation 2(kx + 5) = 6x + 10, k is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["3","4","5","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Expanding the left side gives 2kx + 10. Infinitely many solutions require the x-coefficients to match (2k = 6, so k = 3) and the constants to match (10 = 10).$q$),
  ($q$alg-569$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A taxi company charges $4.00 for the first mile and $1.00 for each additional mile. A passenger paid $11.00 for a ride. How many miles was the ride? (Assume the ride covered a whole number of miles.)$q$, $q$["7","8","9","11"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The first mile costs $4.00, leaving $11.00 − $4.00 = $7.00 for additional miles: 7 additional miles at $1.00. Total distance = 1 + 7 = 8 miles.$q$),
  ($q$alg-570$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$In the equation 3(kx + 7) = 6x + 27, k is a constant. If the equation has no solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["7","2","6","1"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Expanding the left side gives 3kx + 21. No solution requires the x-coefficients to match (3k = 6, so k = 2) while the constants differ (21 ≠ 27).$q$),
  ($q$alg-571$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 6x − 11 = −59, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["−8","8","−12","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$6x = −59 + 11 = −48, so x = −8.$q$),
  ($q$alg-572$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations below has solution (x, y). 3x + y = 0 2x − 2y = −16 What is the value of x + y?$q$, $q$["4","5","−12","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving the system gives x = −2 and y = 6, so x + y = 4.$q$),
  ($q$alg-573$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which ordered pair (x, y) is a solution to the inequality 3x + 3y < 18?$q$, $q$["(8, 0)","(1, 2)","(5, 1)","(−2, 8)"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only (1, 2) works: 3(1) + 3(2) = 9, which is less than 18. The other pairs give values of 18 or more.$q$),
  ($q$alg-574$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A student has at most $50 to spend on notebooks and pens. Notebooks cost $4 each and pens cost $1 each. If the student buys 4 notebooks, what is the maximum number of pens the student can buy?$q$, $q$["33","34","50","35"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$4(4) + 1p ≤ 50 → 1p ≤ 34 → p ≤ 34. The maximum whole number is 34.$q$),
  ($q$alg-575$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A tank contains 460 gallons of water and drains at a constant rate of 8 gallons per minute. After how many minutes will the tank contain 284 gallons?$q$, $q$["20","35","22","176"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$460 − 8t = 284 → 8t = 176 → t = 22.$q$),
  ($q$alg-576$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The line with equation −9x + 12y = −36 crosses the x-axis at (p, 0) and the y-axis at (0, q). What is the value of p + q?$q$, $q$["3","−36","1","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Setting y = 0 gives p = 4. Setting x = 0 gives q = −3. So p + q = 1.$q$),
  ($q$alg-577$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear. If f(0) = −8 and f(4) = 4, what is the value of f(2)?$q$, $q$["−2","2","6","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The slope is (4 − (−8))/(4 − 0) = 3, and f(0) = −8. So f(2) = 3(2) − 8 = −2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-578$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$The graph of 2x + 4y = c passes through the point (1, 6). What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["26","13","−22","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute the point: c = 2(1) + 4(6) = 26.$q$),
  ($q$alg-579$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$In the equation 5(kx + 2) = 15x + 10, k is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["15","4","5","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Expanding the left side gives 5kx + 10. Infinitely many solutions require the x-coefficients to match (5k = 15, so k = 3) and the constants to match (10 = 10).$q$),
  ($q$alg-580$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A student has at most $47 to spend on notebooks and pens. Notebooks cost $3 each and pens cost $1 each. If the student buys 3 notebooks, what is the maximum number of pens the student can buy?$q$, $q$["15","38","39","37"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$3(3) + 1p ≤ 47 → 1p ≤ 38 → p ≤ 38. The maximum whole number is 38.$q$),
  ($q$alg-581$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$In the equation 2(kx + 2) = 8x − 2, k is a constant. If the equation has no solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["4","3","8","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Expanding the left side gives 2kx + 4. No solution requires the x-coefficients to match (2k = 8, so k = 4) while the constants differ (4 ≠ −2).$q$),
  ($q$alg-582$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 12 and x − y = −8, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["12","−8","2","10"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Add the equations: 2x = 4, so x = 2.$q$),
  ($q$alg-583$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$In the equation 5(kx + 3) = 20x + 15, k is a constant. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["5","20","4","3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Expanding the left side gives 5kx + 15. Infinitely many solutions require the x-coefficients to match (5k = 20, so k = 4) and the constants to match (15 = 15).$q$),
  ($q$alg-584$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A taxi company charges $4.00 for the first mile and $3.50 for each additional mile. A passenger paid $53.00 for a ride. How many miles was the ride? (Assume the ride covered a whole number of miles.)$q$, $q$["14","13","15","16"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first mile costs $4.00, leaving $53.00 − $4.00 = $49.00 for additional miles: 14 additional miles at $3.50. Total distance = 1 + 14 = 15 miles.$q$),
